# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
# logrotate policy for this installer's own transcripts.
#
# INSTALLED BY roles/finalize, section 6 of its tasks/main.yml, to
# /etc/logrotate.d/tpot-automation, root-owned 0644. That role reads this
# file, replaces the placeholder in the path below with the log directory
# actually in use -- tpot_log_dir, which --log-dir can move -- and writes the
# result. The substitution is a plain string replace of one literal token and
# nothing else; this file is never interpreted as a template, because a
# logrotate policy is full of braces.
#
# It is shipped as a file rather than written from a heredoc so that a
# packager can install it directly and a reader can see the whole policy
# without reading shell. Nothing else in this tree writes logrotate
# configuration. If it is missing from the installer tree, the role records a
# run warning and installs nothing -- it does not fail the install, because a
# T-Pot that is installed, verified and armed is not a failed install because
# of a rotation policy.
#
# WHAT IS BEING BOUNDED
#   install.sh writes install-<run id>.log and ansible-<run id>.log on every
#   invocation, and roles/tpot_install saves upstream-install-<run>.log
#   beside them. None of those grows once its run has ended -- they
#   ACCUMULATE, one set per run, on a box nobody logs into again. Rotation
#   here is really about putting an upper bound on how many runs' worth of
#   output survives, and the `create 0600 root root` line is the part that
#   matters most: a rotated transcript must not become world-readable,
#   because a transcript is exactly the artefact both of this platform's
#   credential incidents were.
@TPOT_LOG_DIR@/*.log {
    monthly
    rotate 12
    missingok
    notifempty
    compress
    delaycompress
    create 0600 root root
    su root root
}
